## Retrieval: three signals, and why the third exists

<Figure
  title="Recall@8 by retrieval mode"
  caption="Hybrid fusion beats dense alone. Lexical alone is poor on its own, yet removing it from the fusion costs recall — it contributes where embeddings are weakest."
  source="81-case evaluation set, k = 8.">
  <HBar items={recall} max={1} ticks={[0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]}
        format={(v) => v.toFixed(2)} axisLabel="recall@8" labelWidth={120} />
  {#snippet table()}
  <table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>recall@8</th><th>MRR</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Dense</td><td>0.941</td><td>0.862</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lexical</td><td>0.309</td><td>0.270</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hybrid</td><td>0.971</td><td>0.860</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  {/snippet}
</Figure>

<Figure
  title="Mean reciprocal rank by retrieval mode"
  caption="Shown as its own chart rather than a second axis on the one above — two measures on one pair of axes is the chart mistake that makes everything look correlated. Hybrid wins recall and holds MRR level with dense, which is the trade worth making here."
  source="Same evaluation set. Indonesian and English scored within a point of each other.">
  <HBar items={mrr} max={1} ticks={[0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]}
        format={(v) => v.toFixed(2)} axisLabel="MRR" labelWidth={120} />
</Figure>

The three signals are vector similarity, lexical ranking, and **exact overlap on clinical and
product codes**, fused with reciprocal rank fusion. The third exists for a concrete reason: the
text tokeniser normalised a product code and its suffixed variant to the same token, which made
two genuinely different products indistinguishable to lexical search. Rather than fight the
tokeniser, I indexed codes separately and gave exact matches their own ranking.

Embeddings use Matryoshka truncation from 4096 to 1024 dimensions. I did not take that on faith —
a script checks truncated rankings against full-width rankings, and the truncation was adopted
only once they agreed.

<Figure
  title="What the evaluation set is made of"
  caption="Prohibited requests are the largest category, which is deliberate: the costly failure here is answering something the agent should refuse, not missing an FAQ. Multi-turn is highlighted because it is the weak spot — recall@8 of 0.667 against 0.971 overall."
  source="81 cases. Category counts, not weights.">
  <Legend items={[
    { label: 'Categories at or above target', series: 1 },
    { label: 'Multi-turn — known weak spot', series: 2 }
  ]} />
  <HBar items={byCategory} max={18} ticks={[0, 5, 10, 15]}
        format={(v) => String(v)} axisLabel="cases" labelWidth={100} rowHeight={23} />
</Figure>

That 0.667 is written down as the next thing to fix rather than folded into an average: a
follow-up question that depends on earlier context retrieves poorly when embedded on its own.

## Safety as code, not as a judge

The temptation is to ask a model to check the model. I rejected that for the blocking gate, for
two reasons that generalise.

**A guardrail that depends on a ranking function is not a guardrail.** If a safety instruction is
a retrieved chunk, its presence depends on retrieval quality. So the safety context is pinned
into every request regardless of rank.

**A flaky gate gets skipped.** A probabilistic check that fails occasionally for reasons nobody
can reproduce teaches a team to re-run it, and a gate everyone re-runs is not a gate.

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Emergency phrase detection', detail: 'Deterministic, and it runs BEFORE the model is called — not as a review of what the model said. Keyword lists for 13 languages', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Classifiers', detail: 'Urgency, booking intent, lab reading, opt-out. Each documented fail-open or fail-closed by what failure costs. Urgency can only ADD an emergency, never clear one', kind: 'model' },
  { label: 'Retrieval + pinned safety context', detail: 'Safety chunk present regardless of its retrieval rank' },
  { label: 'Agent turn', detail: 'Tool-calling loop. No framework — the medical safety wording had to stay visible to the clinicians who review it', kind: 'model' },
  { label: 'Pure-function checks', detail: 'Grounding (an uncited claim is refused), dose figures, personal medical questions, internal-term blocklist, price citation, formality, message length', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Reply, or handoff to a human' }
]} note="Orange = a model decides. Black = a deterministic gate. The urgency constraint came from testing, where a small model missed an unambiguous chest-pain phrasing in Indonesian." />

A lesson worth recording from the first end-to-end safety run: it reported four failures, and all
four were faulty assertions — the checker was blind to negation and read correct refusals as
violations. After fixing the checker, all twelve passed. The failures were in the test. That is an
argument for reading every failure before acting on the aggregate.

## Order safety

The post-consultation agent can place an order, the highest-risk action in the system. It is
checked twice, independently, in the agent and again in the backend: only drugs on the
prescribing record, never above the prescribed quantity, one order per record. Plan changes go to
the consulting doctor for approval rather than being applied.

Delivery phone numbers are parsed from the patient's own message rather than taken from model
output, because the model once produced a plausible invented number. One medication category
cannot ship outside the metropolitan area, and that check **fails closed** — if the delivery
region cannot be established, it routes to a human.

## Operations

- The safety and unit gate runs in CI **before** the container build and before cloud
  authentication, so a safety failure costs nothing downstream and cannot be bypassed by a
  successful build.
- A nightly end-to-end run against the live model, separate from the deterministic suite.
- An audit record per turn storing setting *names*, never patient values — it proves which
  configuration produced a reply without becoming a second copy of patient data.
- Per-turn traces surfaced in the agent inbox, so a human taking over sees what the model did.
- Provider pinning, after measuring roughly 1 second to first token pinned against about 14.7
  unpinned on the same model.
- Handover documentation records the known gaps too, including that provider-side traces cannot
  be joined to a conversation ID.

## Stack

Python, FastAPI, PostgreSQL with pgvector, hosted models through OpenRouter, Docker, GitHub
Actions, Google Cloud.
